Z||000005||Jake||12-03-2000||10:18 PM||||Yes, that is a good time. I've seen 5.0's do worse||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||12-04-2000||08:16 AM||||HEY JEFF you see this!?  ( GT Bastards) car will pull 14.7 on street tires with STOCK internals on a 2.8 86 SE. I'll see if i don't have a scan oh those runs.. 13.6@103 on 40hp shot of nitrous.   <P><BR>----searching for scans... brb-----||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||12-04-2000||09:55 AM||||Hmmmmm this seems to be a case for Scully and Moulder.  We seem to have 2 Fiero's that are 1.3+ seconds faster then any fiero ever produced.  <P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||12-04-2000||11:54 AM||||4.10 made you faster?? i have heard from 2 people who said it made them slower in the 1/4. IT was good for autocross , but lost time in the 1/4. Steve huskins said it hurt his performance , as did a local friend mark white , which I can attest to , his car gets me for maybe 40-60 feet , then I pull on him....... Both cars had strong 2.8's. Steve's car has a hypertech chip , custom exhaust and MSD ignition. Mark's car has a MSD blaster coil , no cat , Flow master exhuast , short shift  , and a cold air induction kit. Both cars used for autocrosses , but in the 1/4 the gears sure didn't help.<P><BR>Fastest stock 2.8 I have heard of George Balzers 86 GT , 15.2 in the 1/4.<P>Jonathan McCreery||||reg||1
Z||000009||BBBAD GT||12-04-2000||01:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jake:<BR><B>Yes, that is a good time. I've seen 5.0's do worse</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not my 5.0  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I've been once down the 1/8 mi for grins, and did a 10.5 short shifting, which is around 16 sec 1/4 mi, which is average for a stock V6.<P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||82-T/A||12-04-2000||01:23 PM||||Yeah, it's true.. 14.82 at 89.3 miles an hour.<P><BR>The 2.8's internals were all stock..<P>Basically.. here were my mods..<P>No catalytic converter,<BR>Baffles rusted out of muffler.<BR>4.10:1 4-speed from a Chevy Cavalier.<P><BR>I ran with nothing in the trunk, no spare or jack either.<P>My car has NO options... no Spoiler, no trunk release, no defog, no cruise, no power door locks or power windows or power mirrors, AC delco AM/FM radio. <P>I weighed about 185 pounds at the time.<P>I don't see why it would make those guys cars slowerl.. teh 4.10 is awesome.. I've driven a few other stick Fieros.. and this 4.10:1 pulls hard as hell in 4th gear... MASSIVE difference compared to the 3.65:1.. especially in 4th gear.<P>I'm guessing I probably have 300 pounds less of options and body work.. including things like.. trunk fan and stuff like that.<P>Only way that I would see the 4.10s slowing you down is if your trap speed is around 110 miles an hour... cause the car pulls really hard till about that speed.<P>It also had a cracked exhaust manifold.. that when at really high rpms.. sounded like a Formula-1 car.. really sweet.<P>My best launch was with me slipping the clutch to 1,800 rpms and then dropping the hammer.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||12-04-2000||01:33 PM||||pull 300+ lbs of useless stuff out of the car (a/c, related stuff, brackets lines etc, sound deadeners, e-brake, and a host of others) dyno tuning and the proper ign timing and fuel curve any 2.8 (stick)should  run that. the 4:10 made me quicker to 60 but about the same in the 1/4 it doens't have enough HP to push it down the track.. has PLENTY of torque to pull it to 60 but once you in the upper RPM range in 3rd and 4th is all over.. it just runs out of breath...<P><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> heh..  i'm not the only one that pulls crap out of the car i see!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  all i have is my street tire 40hp nitrous  run... i'll e-mail GT Bastard and have him scan and post the 14.7 run.. he has all the time slips. <P>my final tally wad like 287 and some change.. (Ed owner of the shop said "if you gunna take up all this space in my shop for a few days you better document what your doing.." with that said i weighted EVERYTHING i took out of the car.<P>not sure whats up with the 15.2 being the fastest.. we've had this discussion before.. his must have been BONE stock! no exhaust mods or maybe a K&N.. on 400 treadwear tires m+s tires.<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 12-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Black88GT||12-04-2000||03:20 PM||||All I am saying is that its weird with over 150,000 or so V6 Fiero's produced (I guess thats ballpark), one or two happen to run EXTREMELY faster in the Qtr. then the other 149,998 out there.  I don't see how rear defog switches and wires will lose you 50 lbs. either.  PDL's can't add more then 5-10 lbs, same with P/M's.  There is an article somewhere on the net that says even with everything stripped like that you can only lose about 200 lbs.  I think it might have been George Ryan who wrote it, but don't quote me on that.  I think he left most of the interior in there, however.<P>Basically if you say your car lost 300 lbs. or whatever, I would like to see some proof, so I can do it myself!  Not on my GT but maybe a future turbo V6 project car.  300 lbs. is a lot on any car.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||82-T/A||12-04-2000||04:36 PM||||Well, to be more specific....<P>TONS of stuff is missing from the car.<P>Everything from e-brake cables and brackets.. to A/C stuff. A/C worked for a week. I refilled it, and it seized up.. so I removed everything. <P>But remember that my car wasn't stock, the transmission was a 4.10... that wasn't stock.<P>It's also very important that you launch the 4.10s at the right RPMs too.<P>Umm... lets see, the car didn't have any heat shielding... umm... <P>To tell the truth, it was a REAL rat... paint was faded... seats were torn, it was a little rusty under the frame... it was a real beater... I even hit a park fountain with it at like 35 miles an hour.. (long story). <P>It's missing bolts...<P>It's probably lost 100 pounds just from crap thats fallen off of it.<P>I think it's safe to say though that the 4.10 is what made the difference. It was an extremely light car to start with.. but the 4.10s made the difference. I don't remember who on here said that the 4.10s made the car slower.. (not saying your wrong) but everyone I've talked to said it made all the difference in the world.<P>And to be honest, I really don't see how it would make it slower. It should make the car faster in every gear... only reducing the top end of the car by about 8-10 miles an hour.<P>I believe the car topped out at 110 miles an hour. And it got there QUICK...<P>I no longer own it though, I sold it to a guy named Matt Walters.<P>Originally I bought it from John Altman. He was the one that put the new transmission on it and did the valve job before I got it.<P>The car had 163 thousand miles before I sold it to Matt Walters.. he still drives it too...<BR>but he dropped the clutch at 6 grand and snapped the half shafts!!!<P>(got it fixed though)<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000014||82-T/A||12-04-2000||04:38 PM||||Oh yes... forgot to mention,<P>John Altman sold it to me with a set of BRAND NEW Firestone Firehawk SS-10s  215/60/14s all round. <P>Really REALLY nice tires... despite what people say about Firestones. I have a similar set on my other Fiero.<P>And actually, I DEEPLY miss that car... I don't know what I'm going to do.. because, I don't really want my 87 SE / V6... even though it's fully loaded. I really want an all black 1985 Fiero GT 4-Speed... but since the 87 SE / V6 was my first car... it's inconceivable that I could sell it.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000015||88formula||12-04-2000||05:55 PM||||Every time I put in a 4.10 axle ratio in Dragstrip2000 the simulation shows lower 1/4-mile times than the same car with the 3.61 ratio.||||reg||1
Z||000016||82-T/A||12-04-2000||06:11 PM||||I don't necessarily believe in those dragstrip dynoes.<P>They also say that your supposed to drop the clutch at 4 grand. <P>The truth is, those programs.. like Car Test, don't fully know how best to launch the Fiero or at what point to shift at for best acceleration and traction.<P>In my 4.10:1 85 GT.. if I really slammed the gears like it suggested in CarTest... it would peel for like 15 feet in first, seriously lose traction in second, and chirp the tires in third.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000017||Xtreme Fiero's||12-04-2000||10:57 PM||||<BR>Back to the first question, 13.9 @ 100 MPH (my Truck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) I never ran my 84 SE though it was completly gutet t took out the rear carpet, carpet, headliner, dash & center console, pass. seat, door pannels, spare tire & jack I also replaced the decklid with one from a 85 GT witch is alot lighter than stock the car did have a sunroof (removed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) A/C and Power windows, Locks & mirrors I probably got rid of 400 lbs and you could feel a difrence. The car weighed in @ a little over 1900 lbs less driver and fule gauge on E t the local dump i think it read low but still much lighter than stock.<P><BR>Tom D.<BR>1995 Nissan D21<BR>3.0L DOHC V6<BR>Desprately searching for a TD06 turbo from a Typhoon<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||1FST2M6||12-05-2000||07:03 AM||||if you launch at anything above 1800rpm my 3.65 and my 4:10 would wheel hop badly or just spin me around! my best 60' times were dumping the clutch (4 pucks DON'T slip) at about 1300... the weight of the stock flywheel makes for KILLER torque to HURL you past the lights. as for the cartest stuff.. it's not acurate at all... i have dyno print outs of my car, and hours later running it on the track and the numbers were totaly different from what cartest said...  HOWEVER desktop Dyno (engine simulation program) is very accurate! there are too many variables in the cartest program to be close to real world. Driver error is one of the bigest factors. I'm NOT a smooth driver...  but anyone with a little practice can bang the gears halfway decent straight down the 1320. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Jake||12-05-2000||01:12 PM||||Well, I don't know guys. 1fst2m6, U might be right. But I have a friend, Chris, who had an 87 GT 2.8 and the biggest mod on that car was exaust. Jeremy, My other friend, had an 87 5.0 that had a best run of 14.2 but ran 14.5 consistantly. That Fiero would run right beside that 5.0 any day of the week. BUT....Chris got in an accident with his Fiero which totalled it, but the engine and trans were still fine. He wiped that car clean of the suspension, wheels, engine, ect. then ended up buying another 87GT exactly like his old one. He put everything that was on his old GT onto his new one. And now that car is lucky to see a 16 in the 1/4. ||||reg||1
Z||000020||82-T/A||12-05-2000||05:31 PM||||It really depends which year that 5.0 is.<P>They were pretty enemic stock.<P>Only reason why the Mustangs in the 80s were quick was because they were soo light. Most of them came in under 3,000 pounds. Most Fieros weigh around 2,600 pounds. With the average acceleration of a 5.0 Mustang being low 15s and high 14s, it's safe to say that that sounds about right for his quarter mile times... mid 14s.<P>The Fiero V6/60 on the other hand.. while it wasn't enemic for it's size.. it could have been better, but it did have decent torque.<P>Now if you've got that motor, with a 4.10:1 that likes to rev really high, twice the traction, and 200 pounds on average less weight on a pretty basic STOCK Fiero coupe.. your going to get about the same thing.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000021||BBBAD GT||12-06-2000||09:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 82-T/A:<BR><B><BR>Only reason why the Mustangs in the 80s were quick was because they were soo light. Most of them came in under 3,000 pounds. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Most 86-93 Stangs weighed 'over' 3000lb, more like 3200lb.  If you get a striped one with no AC,PW,PL and a notchback, then it might weigh 3000lb even.  Its when the owner puts on draglights for wheels and a glass hood and such that it starts coming in under 3000lb, but I really don't believe you could get one from the factory under 3000lb.  Mine weighs 3400lb with me in it, with glass hood/no ac/drag wheels, but with rollbar/blower/scat sheild/TKO/blah/blah/blah.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||82-T/A||12-06-2000||09:51 AM||||I was actually really referring to the earlier Mustangs. Like 85 and below. <P>I used to have an 82 TransAm, that.. like the Mustangs of that time, were actually pretty light.<P>My 82 TransAm had almost NO options whatsoever.. it really was a piece of crap, but fun to drive never the less. It weighed in just under 3,000 pounds according to the title.<P>The mustangs were a little lighter back then. But not by much. <P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000023||Slammed Fiero||12-06-2000||02:15 PM||||Old 5.0's are dogs , 85 Cars (I believe) were 160Hp , My mom has a 95 5.0ltr convert (#18 from the LAST 5.0ltr Built) has  removeable Hardtop rare rare rare..automatic..Runs low 15's in the 1/4. I still don't understand the 4.10 being faster. I talked to my friend who has a 85 coupe used for autocross , I mentioned him in an earlier post here. He has the battery up front , Dura light race wheels , 4.10 gears , manifold extrude honed , exhaust manifolds cleaned up and a few other things , the car weighs 2451Lbs as per his last autocross. has no options what so ever , spare is gone , His best 1/4 mile time to dat (as of october) was 15.4  somethings wrong at your drag strip Todd His car runs strong and is light...<P>It just doesn't make sense , I ran the #'s through a Vericom (spelling) desktop simulator , commonly used by magazines , I kept getting 15.3 to 15.5 in the 1/4 depending on temp and what not , the 4spd 3.64 ratio tranny (as offered in 85 and 86) with the same mods came up with a 14.9 to 15.1 , I even have exact rear wheel HP for his car and torque specs..<P>Something is fishy..<P>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000024||BBBAD GT||12-06-2000||04:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Old 5.0's are dogs , 85 Cars (I believe) were 160Hp </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I can't let wrong information go on, here are the factory specs on the 5.0's :<P>82 GT (2 bbl carbed):  157 hp<BR>83-84 GT (4 bbl carbed): 175 hp<BR>85 GT (4 bbl carbed):  210 hp<BR>86 GT (SEFI):  200 hp<BR>87-93 GT (SEFI):  225 hp<BR>note that Ford changed the way they report HP, from being a fully balanced stock motor (which yielded greater HP) to a off the assembly line motor to rate the HP.  Nothing internally was changed on the 5.0 from 87-93 (except piston strength) but thier HP rating droped from 225 to like 205 in 1991 or 1992.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000025||82-T/A||12-06-2000||10:35 PM||||"Something is fishy.."<P>As in, I'm full of **** .. <P>heh..<P><BR>Honestly, there isn't anything wrong with Moroso Motorsport Park's track. <P>It's been a while since I sold it, but i'm usually good at remembering numbers. 2535 pounds NET weight according to the title.<P>Now, remove the spare, jack, A/C compressor, catalytic converter, all the emergency brake components... and whatever else fell off from Rust, and you've got a quick car.<P>I'm willing to bet my car ran stronger than his. John Altman had just performed a valve job on the car JUST before I bought it. That of course would include new springs and valves.<P>His motor was probably untouched. <P>Dunno what to tell you, other than sometimes two completely identical cars can run different times. <P>When my 1987 Fiero SE / V6 was running properly.. I used to race it against this 1986 Fiero SE / V6 that I used to own. BOTH cars were EXACTLY the same, even had the same paint.. cept the 86 was a repaint. Everything else on the 86 was original, including the massive original catalytic converter.<P>Now explain this..<P>Both had the GX3 3.33:1 final drive Auto.<P>My 87 would beat the 86 off the line and pull away from it until about 55 miles an hour. At which point... the 86 for some reason would then catch up and then just completely walked away from the 87.... like it was nothing. <P>My 87 was the only car with mods. Ocelot exhaust, Ocelot Cat, and K&N air filter at the time.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000026||GT Bastard||12-07-2000||01:47 AM||||I don't know if I agree with 4.10's being slower.... Top-end, yes, but not 0-60 or even through the 1/4.  Like 1FST mentioned, my car(his old SE) is still running the stock bottom end with bad bearings  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> and it still utterly destroys two local 3.1 4sp Fiero's(both of which are modded to different extents and are running 3.65 trannies.)  We've raced in excess of 80mph, and they don't catch up.||||reg||1
Z||000027||82-T/A||12-07-2000||08:57 AM||||Exactly, I don't know anyone who has owned a 4.10 and said it made their car accelerate slower. Heh, there is nothing that would suggest that otherwise. The higher the gear ratio, the quicker you accelerate. Plain and simple. The lower the gear ratio, the slower you accelerate, and the greater your top end is increased.<P>Unless someone doesn't know how to shift properly or something... I mean, My shifts in that 4 speed were lightning quick... I could have the clutch in and out with it in the next gear before a blink of an eye. <P>Oh man.... I REALLY need to get another Fiero manual. I think I'm going to look for another 4-speed.... I love that transmission.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000028||Slammed Fiero||12-07-2000||10:17 AM||||I will get steve Huskins on This list in the next few Days, he has a 4.10 Gear ratio in his 86 GT , if you can't believe an automotive engineer/ Aerospace engineer which steve is ...who can you believe... And no ...more gear doesn't mean faster accelerating car , it has to do with torque curves and various other factors.<P>I will also scan my dealer brochure on Mustangs , 1985 GT , 160HP is what it says ...<P>Jonathan McCreery||||reg||1
Z||000029||BBBAD GT||12-07-2000||11:36 AM||||I'm sorry, you are right about the 85 GT, mainly because you are refering to an automatic equiped GT.  I keep forgetting about this, but only in 85, was a downgraded version of the 5.0 available with an automatic.  The motor wasn't carbed (as the 5 speeds were), it had some pathetic central fuel injection (CFI) that was poor at best.  The 86-93 5.0's (SEFI) with auto's had the same motor the 5 speeds had.  I'm almost positive an automatic wasn't avail for the 82-84 5.0 GT's.  <P>Sorry for the confusion.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by BBBAD GT (edited 12-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000030||Greg Piet||12-07-2000||02:36 PM||||With regards to all the weight 300lbs being removed...<BR>I just yanked my AC compressor and it was DAMN HEAVY!<P>Arent the power window motors rather heavy as well...<P>now referring to the things such as power trunk etc...  that is just nickel and diming.<P>The amount of fuel in the gas tank is going to make a more pronounced effect.<P>If I could use the last gallon of fuel in my tank, by god I WOULD!<P>Typically I run with about 3-4 gallons in the tank at the autocrosses, namely because when I got lower, and if I were to spin rather violently the car would stall (yes even with the clutch depressed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> )and that may pose a safety hazzard.||||reg||1
Z||000031||82-T/A||12-07-2000||06:06 PM||||No offense, but that doesn't necessarily make him an expert. That would be like me saying I know everything because I own an encyclopedia. <P>Torque curves or nothing... the higher the gear ratio, the more power is going to be delivered to the wheels in a shorter period of time.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000032||Got2manyfieros||12-07-2000||06:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BBBAD GT:<BR><B>Nothing internally was changed on the 5.0 from 87-93 (except piston strength) but thier HP rating droped from 225 to like 205 in 1991 or 1992.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, the 5.0 went from 9.2:1 to 9:1 compression, in 1990, and in 1989 they went to a different cam...many 5.0 mustang owners will tell you how quick an '88 or '87 was compared to 89 and up.||||reg||1
Z||000033||FieroSTETZ||12-07-2000||08:00 PM||||i've driven an 11.63 in a 1963 vw bug. air cooled engine, everything taken out, different cylinders, balanced crank, cam, built with a book called "how to hot rod a vw engine" probably the neatest thing about it was that a 25 shot of nos was recommended, more for cooling than power, and when drag racing a bug, you should use stock style tires, if you grip too much then the engine will bog down. i loved that car, the powerplant lasted about 2-3 runs, then you drop in another, takes about 30 minutes, then about a week to rebuild both engines later- cheapest fun dragracing until i had an explosion. the 11.63 was my first and last time driving that car, i got in trouble and fined- as well as told to leave, since i wore no helmet, had no safety equipment, and was 16- in my pics on photopoint you can see it in the background. my family has 2 bugs, a 350 powered project, and the 11 sec problem. hasn't been run in about 2 years. never run my fiero though... when i do i will let you know whats up<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460599&p=25516673"> ||||reg||1
Z||000034||BBBAD GT||12-08-2000||12:04 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Got2manyfieros:<BR><B> Actually, the 5.0 went from 9.2:1 to 9:1 compression, in 1990, and in 1989 they went to a different cam...many 5.0 mustang owners will tell you how quick an '88 or '87 was compared to 89 and up.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Huh?  The compression stayed at 9:1 from 87-95.  There was no cam change either.  The only reason people say the 87-88 5.0's were faster than the 89+ was just the fact that in 87-88, the computer was a 'speed-density' type (just like the fiero's).  This type infered how much air was entering the motor based on load and other factors.  The 89+ has mass air meters (55mm in size, and restricted the air intake some) that metered the 'actual' air coming into the motor (much more responsive to mods).  I could talk for days about the differences, but this is a Fiero Forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
